  "history": "The Eiffel Tower was built for the 1889 Paris Universal Exposition, held to mark the centenary of the French Revolution. The project was developed by Gustave Eiffel’s company, with structural design by Maurice Koechlin and Émile Nouguier and architectural contributions by Stephen Sauvestre.\nIts wrought-iron components were prefabricated and assembled using rivets. Construction took just over two years, and the tower originally reached approximately 300 metres, making it the tallest structure in the world at the time.\nIt initially drew fierce criticism from artists and intellectuals who felt it disrupted the Parisian landscape. Although conceived as a temporary installation, its scientific uses and later radio transmissions helped secure its survival.\nToday it is an emblem of Paris and a landmark of nineteenth-century metal engineering. Its observation platforms reveal the relationship between the tower, the Seine and the city’s monumental layout.",
