  "history": "The Lincoln Memorial was built in Washington, D.C., between 1914 and 1922 to honour President Abraham Lincoln. Architect Henry Bacon designed a temple directly inspired by ancient Greek architecture.\nThe building is surrounded by Doric columns and contains a large seated statue of Lincoln sculpted by Daniel Chester French. Its interior walls bear inscriptions of important speeches, including the Gettysburg Address.\nAt the western end of the National Mall, the memorial forms a visual axis with the Washington Monument and the Capitol. Its setting beside the Reflecting Pool strengthens the ceremonial character of the ensemble.\nThroughout the twentieth century it hosted civil-rights demonstrations. In 1963, Martin Luther King Jr. delivered his famous “I Have a Dream” speech there, establishing the site as a symbol of memory and democratic advocacy.",
