  "history": "The White House is the official residence and principal workplace of the president of the United States. Construction began in 1792 after an architectural competition won by Irish-born James Hoban, and the building was first occupied in 1800.\nThe design draws on Neoclassical and Palladian traditions, with symmetrical façades, columns and porticoes. Over time, wings, service areas and adaptations were added to accommodate administrative and ceremonial functions.\nDuring the War of 1812, British troops set fire to the building in 1814. Reconstruction preserved its architectural character, although the residence continued to evolve throughout the nineteenth and twentieth centuries.\nThe White House occupies a central place in Washington’s institutional landscape and is internationally recognised as a symbol of the American presidency. Its architecture combines public representation, domestic life and government activity.",
