  "history": "The Library of Celsus was built at Ephesus, in the Roman province of Asia, in the early second century CE. It was commissioned by Gaius Julius Aquila in honour of his father, Tiberius Julius Celsus Polemaeanus, a former governor of the province.\nThe building combined the functions of a library and a funerary monument, since Celsus’s tomb lay beneath it. Its two-storey façade featured columns, niches, sculptures and an elaborate interplay of projections and recesses.\nThe library housed manuscript scrolls kept in cupboards built into the walls. Double-wall construction helped protect the collections from humidity and temperature changes.\nAfter suffering destruction in antiquity, its façade was reconstructed through archaeological work in the twentieth century. Today it is one of Ephesus’s best-known monuments and a landmark of Roman public architecture in Anatolia.",
